</figure><p class="start"><strong>Where to start.</strong> Divide your current GMC premium by your headcount and see which range it falls in.</p><div class="deep-accordion"><details><summary><div class="acc-summary-inner"><div class="acc-summary-label">In full</div><div class="acc-summary-title">What companies spend: the detail</div><div class="acc-summary-sub">1 min read</div></div><span class="deep-acc-indicator" aria-hidden="true">+</span></summary><div class="deep-acc-body"><div class="callout callout-blue"><span aria-hidden="true" class="callout-icon">ℹ</span><div><strong>About this data:</strong> Figures come from the group health policies of 3,500+ companies insured through Plum in FY26. They show the base group medical cover (GMC) premium per employee per year, before life, accident or wellness benefits, rounded and shown as ranges rather than quotes.</div></div><p>The most common range is ₹5,000 to ₹10,000 per employee, where about 3 in 10 companies sit. About 1 in 8 spend over ₹40,000, and more than 9 in 10 of those cover employees' parents.</p><p>Use these ranges as a starting check, not a target. Sitting in the lower quarter is not a problem in itself, and a lean policy can be the right call for a young team. The next two sections show how the picture changes by type of company, and what the highest-spending companies choose to cover.</p></div></details></div></section>
