<section id="what-the-top-quarter-does-differently" class="sec" aria-labelledby="what-the-top-quarter-does-differently-h"><div class="section-label">Part 4 · Policy design</div><h2 id="what-the-top-quarter-does-differently-h">What do the highest-spending companies cover?</h2><p class="section-intro"><strong>The top quarter of companies spend over ₹24,000 per employee, and most of that comes down to policy design.</strong> More than 9 in 10 top-quarter companies cover parents, carry a sum insured of ₹7.5 lakh or more, or both.</p><figure class="cmp-card">
<figcaption>Share of companies with each feature</figcaption>
<div class="cmp-legend"><span class="li"><span class="sw top"></span>Top quarter (over ₹24,000)</span><span class="li"><span class="sw rest"></span>Everyone else</span></div>
<div class="cmp-row"><div class="cmp-label">Cover parents</div>
<div class="cmp-bars"><div class="cmp-bar top" style="width:90%"><span>90%</span></div>
<div class="cmp-bar rest" style="width:11%"><span>11%</span></div></div></div><div class="cmp-row"><div class="cmp-label">Sum insured of ₹7.5 lakh or more</div>
<div class="cmp-bars"><div class="cmp-bar top" style="width:33%"><span>33%</span></div>
<div class="cmp-bar rest" style="width:7%"><span>7%</span></div></div></div><div class="cmp-row"><div class="cmp-label">Include maternity</div>
<div class="cmp-bars"><div class="cmp-bar top" style="width:86%"><span>86%</span></div>
<div class="cmp-bar rest" style="width:55%"><span>55%</span></div></div></div>
</figure><p class="start"><strong>Where to start.</strong> Decide which of these three your employees would use most, then price that one first. <a href="https://www.plumhq.com/get-quote">See where your current policy sits against companies like yours</a>.</p><div class="deep-accordion"><details><summary><div class="acc-summary-inner"><div class="acc-summary-label">In full</div><div class="acc-summary-title">What top spenders cover: the detail</div><div class="acc-summary-sub">1 min read</div></div><span class="deep-acc-indicator" aria-hidden="true">+</span></summary><div class="deep-acc-body"><p>None of these is right for every company. Parental cover adds the most cost, so it is worth knowing how many of your employees would use it. A higher sum insured matters most where a single hospitalisation could exhaust ₹5 lakh. Maternity matters most for a workforce at family-building age. The useful question is which of these your employees would value enough to justify the cost.</p></div></details></div></section><div class="rule-section" aria-hidden="true"><span class="rule-arr-l"></span><span class="rule-line"></span><span class="rule-arr-r"></span></div><div class="chapter-head" id="chapter-3"><span class="ch-num" aria-hidden="true">03</span><div><div class="section-label">Chapter 3 of 6</div><p class="ch-title">How pricing works</p><p class="ch-desc">The inputs insurers price on, and why the same company can land anywhere in the range.</p><ul class="ch-links"><li><a href="#how-group-health-insurance-is-priced-in-india">How GHI is priced</a></li><li><a href="#cost-benchmarks-by-sum-insured">Cost by sum insured</a></li><li><a href="#why-the-same-company-lands-at-different-points-in-the-range">Why quotes vary</a></li><li><a href="#whats-included-in-a-base-policy-and-what-add-ons-cost">Base policy and add-ons</a></li><li><a href="#industry-profile-and-how-it-moves-cost">Industry profile</a></li></ul></div></div><section id="how-group-health-insurance-is-priced-in-india" class="sec" aria-labelledby="how-group-health-insurance-is-priced-in-india-h"><div class="section-label">Part 5 · How pricing works</div><h2 id="how-group-health-insurance-is-priced-in-india-h">How group health insurance is priced in India</h2><p class="section-intro">Insurers price a group health policy using eight main inputs. Any one of these can move the premium by 10 to 50 percent, and the interaction between them is what produces the width of the ranges in this guide.</p><div class="deep-accordion"><details><summary><div class="acc-summary-inner"><div class="acc-summary-label">In full</div><div class="acc-summary-title">How GHI is priced: the detail</div><div class="acc-summary-sub">2 min read</div></div><span class="deep-acc-indicator" aria-hidden="true">+</span></summary><div class="deep-acc-body"><h3>Sum insured</h3><p>The headline cover amount per employee or family floater. Common tiers in the Indian market are ₹1 lakh, ₹2 lakh, ₹3 lakh, ₹5 lakh, ₹7.5 lakh, and ₹10 lakh and above. Doubling the sum insured does not double the premium; the relationship is closer to a 40 to 60 percent uplift at each step because claims severity concentrates at the lower end of the hospitalisation cost distribution.</p><h3>Family definition</h3><p>The combination of dependents covered. The main configurations are:</p><ul>
<li><strong>E</strong>: employee only</li>
<li><strong>E+S</strong>: employee and spouse</li>
<li><strong>E+S+C</strong>: employee, spouse, and children</li>
<li><strong>E+S+C+P</strong>: employee, spouse, children, and parents (or parents-in-law)</li>
</ul><p>Moving from E to E+S+C typically adds 60 to 110 percent to premium. Adding parents can add another 40 to 120 percent on top of that, depending on parent age bands and whether the cover is floater or individual.</p><h3>Age and gender profile of the covered population</h3><p>A workforce with a median age of 28 will price materially lower than one with a median age of 40, and a workforce with higher female representation will price higher for policies that include maternity. Insurers model age-bracket distributions, not averages, so a bimodal workforce (many under-30s and a leadership layer in their 50s) prices differently from a flat profile at the same mean age.</p><h3>Industry and occupational risk</h3><p>Office-based roles sit at the low end of the risk curve. Manufacturing, logistics, field sales, and construction sit higher. Insurers apply loading of 5 to 25 percent for industries with elevated accident or occupational-illness profiles. Tech, BFSI, and professional services companies therefore see a different pricing floor than light manufacturing of the same size.</p><h3>Claims history (from renewal year 2 onward)</h3><p>For the first-year policy, insurers price on employee demographics and policy design. From the second renewal, actual claims experience takes over as the dominant input. A company with a loss ratio above 85 percent in year 1 should expect a 20 to 45 percent renewal hike in year 2, independent of market movement. A loss ratio under 50 percent can hold renewal increases to 5 to 15 percent.</p><h3>Add-ons and riders</h3><p>Maternity benefit, parental cover, OPD, mental health, modern treatments, infertility cover, critical illness riders, and personal accident each have their own cost structure. The impact of each as a percentage of base premium is covered in a dedicated section below.</p><h3>Geography and hospital network</h3><p>Metro-heavy workforces price higher than tier-2 or tier-3 heavy workforces because urban hospital tariffs are higher. The cashless hospital network available to a given policy depends on the insurer; Plum works with multiple insurers including ICICI Lombard, HDFC ERGO, Bajaj Allianz, Star Health, Niva Bupa, and Aditya Birla Health Insurance, each with distinct network footprints.</p><h3>Group size</h3><p>Larger groups get better rates because of risk pooling and broker leverage with insurers. Plum writes GHI policies starting at a minimum group size of 7 employees, which is lower than most market defaults. The pricing effect of group size is explored in the next section.</p></div></details></div></section>
<div class="rule-section lav" aria-hidden="true"><span class="rule-arr-l"></span><span class="rule-line"></span><span class="rule-arr-r"></span></div>
<section id="cost-benchmarks-by-sum-insured" class="sec" aria-labelledby="cost-benchmarks-by-sum-insured-h"><div class="section-label">Part 6 · Sum insured</div><h2 id="cost-benchmarks-by-sum-insured-h">Cost benchmarks by sum insured</h2><p class="section-intro">The table below shows indicative PEPY ranges for the same E+S+C family definition at different sum insured tiers, for a mid-size company (51 to 500 employees) in a low-risk industry.</p><div class="table-wrap" tabindex="0"><table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Sum insured per family</th>
<th>Indicative PEPY range</th>
<th>Typical use case</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td>₹1 lakh</td>
