<td>Entry-level cover, often paired with ESIC</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>₹3 lakh</td>
<td>₹4,500 to ₹8,500</td>
<td>Common SMB and startup baseline</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>₹5 lakh</td>
<td>₹6,500 to ₹12,000</td>
<td>Standard mid-market cover</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>₹7.5 lakh</td>
<td>₹9,000 to ₹16,000</td>
<td>Richer mid-market and tech companies</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>₹10 lakh</td>
<td>₹12,000 to ₹20,000</td>
<td>Senior-weighted workforces, leadership tiers</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>₹10 lakh+</td>
<td>₹15,000 to ₹30,000+</td>
<td>Leadership-only or super top-up structures</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table></div><div class="deep-accordion"><details><summary><div class="acc-summary-inner"><div class="acc-summary-label">In full</div><div class="acc-summary-title">Cost by sum insured: the detail</div><div class="acc-summary-sub">1 min read</div></div><span class="deep-acc-indicator" aria-hidden="true">+</span></summary><div class="deep-acc-body"><p>The per-rupee cost of cover drops as sum insured rises. A ₹10 lakh policy does not cost five times a ₹2 lakh policy because the probability of a claim exceeding ₹2 lakh is much lower than the probability of any claim at all.</p></div></details></div></section>
<div class="rule-section lav" aria-hidden="true"><span class="rule-arr-l"></span><span class="rule-line"></span><span class="rule-arr-r"></span></div>
<section id="why-the-same-company-lands-at-different-points-in-the-range" class="sec" aria-labelledby="why-the-same-company-lands-at-different-points-in-the-range-h"><div class="section-label">Part 7 · What moves your quote</div><h2 id="why-the-same-company-lands-at-different-points-in-the-range-h">Why the same company lands at different points in the range</h2><p class="section-intro">A single 150-person tech company in Bengaluru can get quoted anywhere from ₹5,500 to ₹13,000 PEPY for what sounds like the same policy. The illustration below shows the levers that produce that spread for the same company.</p><div class="table-wrap" tabindex="0"><table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Scenario</th>
<th>PEPY</th>
<th>What's different</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td>Low end</td>
<td>~₹5,500</td>
<td>₹3 lakh SI, E only, no maternity, 25 percent co-pay, ₹5,000 room-rent cap, 2-year disease waiting period kept</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Mid</td>
<td>~₹8,500</td>
<td>₹5 lakh SI, E+S+C, maternity included, no co-pay, single private room, waiting periods waived</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>High end</td>
<td>~₹13,000</td>
<td>₹5 lakh SI, E+S+C, maternity + parental (one parent) + OPD, no co-pay, no sub-limits, modern treatments rider</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table></div><div class="deep-accordion"><details><summary><div class="acc-summary-inner"><div class="acc-summary-label">In full</div><div class="acc-summary-title">Why quotes vary: the detail</div><div class="acc-summary-sub">1 min read</div></div><span class="deep-acc-indicator" aria-hidden="true">+</span></summary><div class="deep-acc-body"><p>The low-end policy is cheaper on the invoice. It also pushes substantial cost onto employees at the moment of claim, which is covered in a later section.</p></div></details></div></section>
<div class="rule-section lav" aria-hidden="true"><span class="rule-arr-l"></span><span class="rule-line"></span><span class="rule-arr-r"></span></div>
<section id="whats-included-in-a-base-policy-and-what-add-ons-cost" class="sec" aria-labelledby="whats-included-in-a-base-policy-and-what-add-ons-cost-h"><div class="section-label">Part 8 · Coverage and add-ons</div><h2 id="whats-included-in-a-base-policy-and-what-add-ons-cost-h">What's included in a base policy and what add-ons cost</h2><p class="section-intro">A base GHI policy typically includes in-patient hospitalisation (minimum 24 hours), day-care procedures, pre-hospitalisation expenses (30 to 60 days), post-hospitalisation expenses (60 to 90 days), ambulance charges, and domiciliary hospitalisation under defined conditions. ICU, room rent, and surgeon fees are included within sub-limits that the policy design specifies.</p><div class="table-wrap" tabindex="0"><table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Add-on</th>
<th>Uplift on base premium</th>
<th>Notes</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td>Maternity benefit</td>
<td>+10% to +25%</td>
<td>Varies with waiting period, cap amount, and infant cover duration</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Parental cover (floater, both parents)</td>
<td>+40% to +120%</td>
<td>Highest-impact add-on; see dedicated section below</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>OPD cover</td>
<td>+8% to +18%</td>
<td>Wallet model usually cheaper than claim-reimbursement model</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Mental health cover</td>
<td>+2% to +6%</td>
<td>Mandatory IRDAI inclusion for in-patient; OPD mental health is add-on</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Modern treatments rider</td>
<td>+1% to +3%</td>
<td>Robotic surgery, oral chemotherapy, stem-cell therapy, 10 to 12 named procedures</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Infertility cover</td>
<td>+3% to +8%</td>
<td>IVF and ART; typically capped and age-restricted</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Personal accident cover</td>
<td>+2% to +5%</td>
<td>Separate benefit, not a loading on base</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Critical illness rider</td>
<td>+4% to +10%</td>
<td>Lump-sum benefit on diagnosis of listed conditions</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table></div><div class="deep-accordion"><details><summary><div class="acc-summary-inner"><div class="acc-summary-label">In full</div><div class="acc-summary-title">Base policy and add-ons: the detail</div><div class="acc-summary-sub">1 min read</div></div><span class="deep-acc-indicator" aria-hidden="true">+</span></summary><div class="deep-acc-body"><p>Add-ons sit on top. The ranges below show the typical uplift as a percentage of base premium for each add-on.</p><p>Companies evaluating add-ons should map each one to actual employee utilisation data from the previous year, if available, rather than selecting from the menu by intuition.</p></div></details></div></section>
<div class="rule-section lav" aria-hidden="true"><span class="rule-arr-l"></span><span class="rule-line"></span><span class="rule-arr-r"></span></div>
<section id="industry-profile-and-how-it-moves-cost" class="sec" aria-labelledby="industry-profile-and-how-it-moves-cost-h"><div class="section-label">Part 9 · Industry</div><h2 id="industry-profile-and-how-it-moves-cost-h">Industry profile and how it moves cost</h2><div class="deep-accordion"><details><summary><div class="acc-summary-inner"><div class="acc-summary-label">In full</div><div class="acc-summary-title">Industry profile: the detail</div><div class="acc-summary-sub">1 min read</div></div><span class="deep-acc-indicator" aria-hidden="true">+</span></summary><div class="deep-acc-body"><ul>
<li><strong>Technology and SaaS</strong>: sits at the baseline. Low-risk occupation, young workforce, metro-concentrated. The PEPY ranges in the tables above apply directly.</li>
<li><strong>BFSI</strong>: similar to tech on occupational risk, but older median age in traditional banking segments shifts PEPY 10 to 20 percent higher.</li>
<li><strong>Professional services (consulting, legal, accounting)</strong>: tech-adjacent for occupational risk, with higher travel and stress-related claim patterns that show up at year-2 renewal.</li>
<li><strong>Manufacturing and industrial</strong>: 15 to 25 percent loading on PEPY compared to tech, driven by accident risk and higher incidence of back and joint claims.</li>
<li><strong>Logistics and field sales</strong>: 10 to 20 percent loading, with policy design often needing personal accident cover as a mandatory layer.</li>
<li><strong>Healthcare and pharma</strong>: variable; office-based pharma prices as tech, hospital staff prices as manufacturing.</li>
<li><strong>Retail and hospitality</strong>: moderate loading, with higher attrition driving administrative load that affects total cost of ownership.</li>
</ul></div></details></div></section><div class="rule-section" aria-hidden="true"><span class="rule-arr-l"></span><span class="rule-line"></span><span class="rule-arr-r"></span></div><div class="chapter-head" id="chapter-4"><span class="ch-num" aria-hidden="true">04</span><div><div class="section-label">Chapter 4 of 6</div><p class="ch-title">The full cost</p><p class="ch-desc">What the premium leaves out: renewals, risk transfer, peer benchmarks and admin load.</p><ul class="ch-links"><li><a href="#beyond-the-sticker-price-three-cost-realities-most-buyers-mi">Beyond the sticker price: three cost realities most buyers miss</a></li><li><a href="#the-cfo-view-group-health-insurance-as-risk-transfer">The CFO view</a></li><li><a href="#benchmarking-against-the-right-peer-set">Peer-set benchmarking</a></li><li><a href="#admin-load-and-total-cost-of-ownership">Admin load and TCO</a></li></ul></div></div><section id="beyond-the-sticker-price-three-cost-realities-most-buyers-mi" class="sec" aria-labelledby="beyond-the-sticker-price-three-cost-realities-most-buyers-mi-h"><div class="section-label">Part 10 · Hidden costs</div><h2 id="beyond-the-sticker-price-three-cost-realities-most-buyers-mi-h">Beyond the sticker price: three cost realities most buyers miss</h2><p class="section-intro">The premium printed on an insurer quote is one number. The actual cost of operating a group health insurance programme over three years is a different number. Three realities drive the gap.</p><div class="table-wrap" tabindex="0"><table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Design choice to cut premium</th>
<th>Premium saving</th>
<th>What the employee pays at claim time</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td>Add 20% co-pay on all claims</td>
<td>10% to 15%</td>
<td>20% of every hospitalisation bill, cash at discharge</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Add ₹5,000 room-rent cap</td>
<td>5% to 10%</td>
<td>Proportional deduction on all associated bills if they pick a higher-category room</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Keep 2-year disease-waiting period</td>
<td>3% to 7%</td>
<td>Full out-of-pocket cost for listed conditions in first 2 years</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Add 10% disease-wise sub-limits (cataract, hernia, etc.)</td>
<td>2% to 5%</td>
<td>Any cost above the sub-limit cap</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Exclude maternity</td>
<td>10% to 25%</td>
<td>Full delivery cost (₹60,000 to ₹3 lakh) or shift to spouse's policy</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table></div><div class="deep-accordion"><details><summary><div class="acc-summary-inner"><div class="acc-summary-label">In full</div><div class="acc-summary-title">Beyond the sticker price: three cost realities most buyers miss: the detail</div><div class="acc-summary-sub">1 min read</div></div><span class="deep-acc-indicator" aria-hidden="true">+</span></summary><div class="deep-acc-body"><h3>Year 3 is priced by your year 1 design choices</h3><p>Claims experience is the dominant input to year 2 and year 3 pricing, and claims experience is shaped by year 1 design choices. A company that buys the cheapest possible first-year policy, with high co-pays and tight sub-limits, often sees worse claims outcomes (because employees delay care, or because claims hit cost caps and get partially denied) and still ends up with high renewal hikes because the loss ratio reads badly on paper.</p><p>A buyer picking between a ₹5,500 PEPY policy and a ₹7,500 PEPY policy for the same company should model the three-year total, not just year 1. The cheaper policy frequently ends up more expensive by year 3.</p><h3>Cost drifts after the policy starts</h3><p>The PEPY you sign for is the opening balance. Mid-year endorsements add 10 to 20 percent to the annual outlay for most growing companies.</p><ul>
<li><strong>New joiner additions</strong>: pro-rated against the annual premium, often at a slightly higher per-month rate than the base.</li>
<li><strong>Mid-term dependent additions</strong> (marriages, births, newly covered parents): typically full annual premium for the dependent, not pro-rated.</li>
<li><strong>Mid-year sum insured upgrades</strong>: full differential charged, with underwriting at the new tier.</li>
<li><strong>Add-on mid-term activation</strong> (new OPD wallet, extended maternity): full differential plus any administration fee the broker or insurer charges.</li>
</ul><p>A finance team that budgets at the quoted PEPY and does not reserve 10 to 20 percent for in-year drift typically ends the policy year over budget.</p><h3>When you cut premium, you transfer cost to employees</h3><p>Every rupee shaved off the premium gets paid by someone. For design choices that cut premium, the person paying is the employee at the moment of claim.</p><p>A premium cut via co-pay and sub-limits is not a cost reduction. It is a transfer from the employer's P&amp;L to the employee's bank account, triggered at the worst possible moment for the employee.</p></div></details></div></section>
<div class="rule-section lav" aria-hidden="true"><span class="rule-arr-l"></span><span class="rule-line"></span><span class="rule-arr-r"></span></div>
<section id="the-cfo-view-group-health-insurance-as-risk-transfer" class="sec" aria-labelledby="the-cfo-view-group-health-insurance-as-risk-transfer-h"><div class="section-label">Part 11 · For finance teams</div><h2 id="the-cfo-view-group-health-insurance-as-risk-transfer-h">The CFO view: group health insurance as risk transfer</h2><p class="section-intro">A CFO framing of GHI treats the premium as the price of transferring tail risk. The question is not "what does GHI cost per employee" but "what is the probability-weighted cost of absorbing catastrophic health events on the balance sheet versus paying a known annual premium to transfer that risk."</p><div class="deep-accordion"><details><summary><div class="acc-summary-inner"><div class="acc-summary-label">In full</div><div class="acc-summary-title">The CFO view: the detail</div><div class="acc-summary-sub">1 min read</div></div><span class="deep-acc-indicator" aria-hidden="true">+</span></summary><div class="deep-acc-body"><p>For a 100-person company:</p><ul>
<li>Expected hospitalisation incidence per year: 6 to 12 events</li>
<li>Expected high-severity events (above ₹5 lakh): 0 to 2 per year</li>
<li>Expected catastrophic events (above ₹15 lakh): a non-zero probability every 2 to 4 years</li>
<li>Without GHI: these costs land on the employer (if absorbed voluntarily) or on employees (if not), with the second case producing substantial attrition and productivity cost</li>
</ul><p>The GHI premium at 100 people (roughly ₹5 lakh to ₹12 lakh for a baseline policy) is small relative to the variance it absorbs. For a CFO, the premium range is less interesting than the smoothness of the P&amp;L outcome and the insulation from a bad-luck quarter where one leader needs cardiac surgery and two employees have complicated deliveries.</p></div></details></div></section>
<div class="rule-section lav" aria-hidden="true"><span class="rule-arr-l"></span><span class="rule-line"></span><span class="rule-arr-r"></span></div>
<section id="benchmarking-against-the-right-peer-set" class="sec" aria-labelledby="benchmarking-against-the-right-peer-set-h"><div class="section-label">Part 12 · Benchmarking</div><h2 id="benchmarking-against-the-right-peer-set-h">Benchmarking against the right peer set</h2><p class="section-intro">Market averages are the wrong benchmark for most companies. The right benchmark is a peer set of five to ten companies with similar stage, city, hiring pool, and employee value proposition — the companies this one actually competes with for talent.</p><div class="deep-accordion"><details><summary><div class="acc-summary-inner"><div class="acc-summary-label">In full</div><div class="acc-summary-title">Peer-set benchmarking: the detail</div><div class="acc-summary-sub">1 min read</div></div><span class="deep-acc-indicator" aria-hidden="true">+</span></summary><div class="deep-acc-body"><p>Peer-set benchmarking is tighter than market-average benchmarking. A 200-person Series B SaaS company in Bengaluru competing with other Series B SaaS companies for engineering talent should expect its GHI to sit within a ₹2,000 to ₹3,000 PEPY band of its peers, even though the market range for 51 to 200 employee companies spans ₹4,500 to ₹8,500.</p><p>A buyer whose policy sits in the market range but 25 percent below their peer set has a retention risk that will not show up in a benchmarking report. One whose policy sits above their peer set has a budget conversation to have with their CFO.</p><p>The right questions to answer when building a peer set:</p><ul>
<li>Which 10 companies do we lose candidates to or win candidates from?</li>
<li>What is the median tenure of the engineering or sales team at those companies?</li>
<li>What is the published or signalled GHI structure at those companies (sum insured, family definition, parental cover presence)?</li>
</ul></div></details></div></section>
<div class="rule-section lav" aria-hidden="true"><span class="rule-arr-l"></span><span class="rule-line"></span><span class="rule-arr-r"></span></div>
<section id="admin-load-and-total-cost-of-ownership" class="sec" aria-labelledby="admin-load-and-total-cost-of-ownership-h"><div class="section-label">Part 13 · Total cost of ownership</div><h2 id="admin-load-and-total-cost-of-ownership-h">Admin load and total cost of ownership</h2><p class="section-intro">Premium is one line of the GHI cost. HR hours spent operating the programme are another. For a 100-person company, a well-run programme consumes 3 to 6 HR hours per month on steady state and 15 to 25 hours per month during renewal and open enrolment. A poorly-run programme can consume 15 to 20 hours per month on steady state alone, driven by:</p><div class="deep-accordion"><details><summary><div class="acc-summary-inner"><div class="acc-summary-label">In full</div><div class="acc-summary-title">Admin load and TCO: the detail</div><div class="acc-summary-sub">1 min read</div></div><span class="deep-acc-indicator" aria-hidden="true">+</span></summary><div class="deep-acc-body"><ul>
<li>Employee queries about coverage (what's included, how to claim, which hospitals)</li>
<li>Endorsement management (new joiners, dependent additions, exits)</li>
<li>Claim escalations and insurer follow-up</li>
<li>Pre-authorisation chasing during active hospitalisations</li>
</ul><p>At an average loaded HR cost of ₹1,200 to ₹2,500 per hour, the gap between a well-run and poorly-run programme is ₹20,000 to ₹60,000 per month, or ₹2.4 lakh to ₹7.2 lakh per year on admin load alone. That number should factor into any TCO comparison across brokers.</p><p>Service quality shows up in two metrics in particular: pre-authorisation turnaround time and claim settlement experience. Plum's median pre-authorisation TAT is 45 minutes, and Plum's claims NPS is 79, both of which are above typical industry benchmarks and both of which translate directly into fewer HR escalations.</p></div></details></div></section><div class="rule-section" aria-hidden="true"><span class="rule-arr-l"></span><span class="rule-line"></span><span class="rule-arr-r"></span></div><div class="chapter-head" id="chapter-5"><span class="ch-num" aria-hidden="true">05</span><div><div class="section-label">Chapter 5 of 6</div><p class="ch-title">Levers you control</p><p class="ch-desc">Parental cover, ESIC, tiering and how to set a budget.</p><ul class="ch-links"><li><a href="#parental-cover-the-fastest-growing-and-most-under-provisione">Parental cover: the fastest-growing and most under-provisioned line</a></li><li><a href="#esic-and-group-health-insurance-together">ESIC and GHI</a></li><li><a href="#tiering-as-a-cost-lever">Tiering</a></li><li><a href="#how-to-budget-for-group-health-insurance">How to budget</a></li></ul></div></div><section id="parental-cover-the-fastest-growing-and-most-under-provisione" class="sec" aria-labelledby="parental-cover-the-fastest-growing-and-most-under-provisione-h"><div class="section-label">Part 14 · Parental cover</div><h2 id="parental-cover-the-fastest-growing-and-most-under-provisione-h">Parental cover: the fastest-growing and most under-provisioned line</h2><p class="section-intro">Parental cover is the single most impactful add-on on premium and the one that most scaling companies get wrong. Three structural facts drive the economics:</p><div class="deep-accordion"><details><summary><div class="acc-summary-inner"><div class="acc-summary-label">In full</div><div class="acc-summary-title">Parental cover: the fastest-growing and most under-provisioned line: the detail</div><div class="acc-summary-sub">1 min read</div></div><span class="deep-acc-indicator" aria-hidden="true">+</span></summary><div class="deep-acc-body"><h3>Claim severity is 3 to 5 times higher for parents</h3><p>Parent-age populations (55 to 75) generate claims that are both more frequent and larger than employee or dependent-child claims. A parent cohort can account for 50 to 70 percent of a policy's total claim payout while representing 20 to 30 percent of covered lives.</p><h3>Renewal hikes concentrate here</h3><p>A policy with uncapped parental cover frequently sees year-2 renewal increases of 35 to 60 percent, almost entirely driven by the parent segment. Policies without parental cover see much more stable renewal pricing.</p><h3>Structuring options control cost without cutting the benefit</h3><p>Companies that want to offer parental cover without absorbing full cost have several design paths:</p><ul>
<li><strong>Voluntary / employee-paid</strong>: employer makes the policy available, employee pays the parental cover premium</li>
<li><strong>Buy-up structure</strong>: employer funds a base amount, employee tops up for parental inclusion</li>
<li><strong>Tiered sum insured</strong>: lower sum insured for parents than for employee and immediate family</li>
<li><strong>Age-banded premium sharing</strong>: employer funds a higher share for parents under 60, lower share above</li>
<li><strong>Capped out-patient and modern-treatment exclusions on the parent segment</strong></li>
</ul><p>The right structure depends on workforce demographics, employee preferences, and the signal the company wants to send about care for extended family. There is no universally correct answer.</p></div></details></div></section>
<div class="rule-section lav" aria-hidden="true"><span class="rule-arr-l"></span><span class="rule-line"></span><span class="rule-arr-r"></span></div>
<section id="esic-and-group-health-insurance-together" class="sec" aria-labelledby="esic-and-group-health-insurance-together-h"><div class="section-label">Part 15 · ESIC</div><h2 id="esic-and-group-health-insurance-together-h">ESIC and group health insurance together</h2><p class="section-intro">Employees with monthly wages up to ₹21,000 are mandatorily covered under the Employees' State Insurance Corporation (ESIC) scheme. Companies with mixed wage bands often need to run ESIC for the covered segment and private GHI for the rest, which raises three questions:</p><div class="deep-accordion"><details><summary><div class="acc-summary-inner"><div class="acc-summary-label">In full</div><div class="acc-summary-title">ESIC and GHI: the detail</div><div class="acc-summary-sub">1 min read</div></div><span class="deep-acc-indicator" aria-hidden="true">+</span></summary><div class="deep-acc-body"><h3>Do I need private GHI on top of ESIC for ESIC-covered employees?</h3><p>Not legally required. Many companies choose to provide a top-up private GHI for ESIC-covered employees because ESIC hospital access is uneven across cities and procedure types, and private cover produces materially better employee experience during claims.</p><h3>How much does private cover for the ESIC-covered segment cost?</h3><p>Typically ₹1,500 to ₹4,000 PEPY for a ₹1 lakh to ₹2 lakh sum insured top-up. Lower sum insured policies have proportionally higher per-rupee premiums because of fixed administrative costs.</p><h3>Should I combine both populations into one GHI policy?</h3><p>Operationally simpler, financially more expensive. A unified policy at ₹3 lakh sum insured for everyone can cost 20 to 40 percent more than a split structure. Larger companies with HR capacity usually run split structures; smaller companies often prioritise simplicity.</p></div></details></div></section>
<div class="rule-section lav" aria-hidden="true"><span class="rule-arr-l"></span><span class="rule-line"></span><span class="rule-arr-r"></span></div>
<section id="tiering-as-a-cost-lever" class="sec" aria-labelledby="tiering-as-a-cost-lever-h"><div class="section-label">Part 16 · Cost levers</div><h2 id="tiering-as-a-cost-lever-h">Tiering as a cost lever</h2><p class="section-intro">Treating "the GHI policy" as monolithic is a common and expensive mistake. Companies that tier their GHI structure can hold total employer cost flat while materially improving perceived benefit. Three common tiering patterns:</p><div class="deep-accordion"><details><summary><div class="acc-summary-inner"><div class="acc-summary-label">In full</div><div class="acc-summary-title">Tiering: the detail</div><div class="acc-summary-sub">1 min read</div></div><span class="deep-acc-indicator" aria-hidden="true">+</span></summary><div class="deep-acc-body"><h3>Role-based tiers</h3><p>Different sum insured and family definition for leadership (VP+ or founders), management (senior IC and manager), and individual contributors. Leadership tier typically has ₹10 lakh to ₹25 lakh sum insured with full parental cover; IC tier might have ₹3 lakh to ₹5 lakh sum insured with E+S+C only. Total employer cost can be 10 to 20 percent lower than offering the leadership policy to everyone.</p><h3>Voluntary top-ups</h3><p>Base policy covers everyone at a sensible floor (say ₹3 lakh SI, E+S+C), with employee-paid voluntary top-ups available for higher sum insured, parental inclusion, or OPD. The employer absorbs the base cost; the employee funds personalisation. Participation rates in voluntary top-ups typically run 30 to 55 percent among eligible employees.</p><h3>Dependent buy-ups</h3><p>Base policy covers employee only or E+S, with employees able to buy additional dependents at a defined contribution split. Common in cost-conscious startups transitioning from E-only to E+S+C.</p><p>The design choice signals values. A company that funds parental cover for everyone signals extended-family care. One that offers it as a voluntary buy-up signals personal choice. Neither is correct in the abstract; both are correct for the company whose values they reflect.</p></div></details></div></section>
<div class="rule-section lav" aria-hidden="true"><span class="rule-arr-l"></span><span class="rule-line"></span><span class="rule-arr-r"></span></div>
<section id="how-to-budget-for-group-health-insurance" class="sec" aria-labelledby="how-to-budget-for-group-health-insurance-h"><div class="section-label">Part 17 · Budgeting</div><h2 id="how-to-budget-for-group-health-insurance-h">How to budget for group health insurance</h2><p class="section-intro">Three budgeting approaches, in increasing order of sophistication:</p><ol class="steps"><li class="step"><h3>Rule of thumb: percentage of CTC</h3><p>Most Indian companies spend 0.8 to 2.5 percent of total CTC on GHI premium. Tech and professional services companies cluster at 1.2 to 1.8 percent. This method is quick but imprecise, because CTC varies with city and seniority mix in ways that do not perfectly track hospitalisation risk.</p></li><li class="step"><h3>Peer-set benchmarking</h3><p>Build a peer set of five to ten similar companies and target the median of that peer set, with explicit decisions about where to sit above or below.</p></li><li class="step"><h3>Design-first budgeting</h3><p>Start from the benefit the company wants to offer (sum insured, family definition, add-ons) and work backward to the premium. This is the right method for companies that treat GHI as a strategic lever rather than an expense line, and it is the one that typically produces the best employee outcomes.</p></li></ol><div class="deep-accordion"><details><summary><div class="acc-summary-inner"><div class="acc-summary-label">In full</div><div class="acc-summary-title">How to budget: the detail</div><div class="acc-summary-sub">1 min read</div></div><span class="deep-acc-indicator" aria-hidden="true">+</span></summary><div class="deep-acc-body"><div class="callout callout-coral"><span aria-hidden="true" class="callout-icon">⚑</span><div><strong>Budget note:</strong> A full budget should include three line items: premium, administration (HR hours), and a 10 to 20 percent reserve for mid-year drift.</div></div></div></details></div></section><div class="rule-section" aria-hidden="true"><span class="rule-arr-l"></span><span class="rule-line"></span><span class="rule-arr-r"></span></div><div class="chapter-head" id="chapter-6"><span class="ch-num" aria-hidden="true">06</span><div><div class="section-label">Chapter 6 of 6</div><p class="ch-title">Quick reference</p><p class="ch-desc">The headline numbers and answers to the questions buyers ask most.</p><ul class="ch-links"><li><a href="#by-the-numbers">At a glance</a></li><li><a href="#frequently-asked-questions">FAQs</a></li></ul></div></div><section id="by-the-numbers" class="sec" aria-labelledby="by-the-numbers-h"><div class="section-label">Benchmarks</div><h2 id="by-the-numbers-h">Group health insurance spend at a glance</h2><div class="stat-row"><div class="stat-box"><span class="stat-unit">per employee a year</span><div class="stat-num">₹6,000–24,000</div><div class="stat-label">What half of companies spend on group health insurance.</div></div><div class="stat-box"><span class="stat-unit">policies</span><div class="stat-num">3 in 10</div><div class="stat-label">Cover employees' parents, a share that has held steady since FY22.</div></div><div class="stat-box"><span class="stat-unit">policies</span><div class="stat-num">6 in 10</div><div class="stat-label">Carry a sum insured of ₹5–7.5 lakh, the most common band.</div></div><div class="stat-box"><span class="stat-unit">top-quarter spenders</span><div class="stat-num">9 in 10</div><div class="stat-label">Cover parents, carry ₹7.5 lakh or more, or both.</div></div></div><p class="src">Source: Plum customer data, FY22 to FY26.</p><a class="report-card" href="https://www.plumhq.com/standard-of-employee-benefits">
<span class="badge badge-mandatory">The latest edition</span>
<span class="rc-title">The Standard of Employee Benefits 2026–27</span>
<span class="rc-desc">Plum's benchmark report on what funded startups, Indian businesses and MNCs actually offer, from 15,312 benefit plans.</span>
<span class="rc-link">Read the report →</span></a></section>
<div class="rule-section lav" aria-hidden="true"><span class="rule-arr-l"></span><span class="rule-line"></span><span class="rule-arr-r"></span></div>
<section id="frequently-asked-questions" class="sec" aria-labelledby="frequently-asked-questions-h"><div class="section-label">Quick answers</div><h2 id="frequently-asked-questions-h">Frequently asked questions</h2><p class="section-intro">The questions founders, HR and finance teams ask most about group health insurance cost.</p><div class="deep-accordion faq-acc"><details><summary><div class="acc-summary-inner"><h3 class="acc-summary-title">How much does group health insurance cost per employee in India?</h3></div><span class="deep-acc-indicator" aria-hidden="true">+</span></summary><div class="deep-acc-body"><p>Across 3,500+ companies insured through Plum, half spend roughly ₹6,000 to ₹24,000 per employee per year. Where a company lands depends on sum insured, family definition, age mix, industry, and add-ons. A baseline ₹3 lakh sum insured policy with E+S+C family definition for a mid-size tech company usually prices between ₹4,500 and ₹8,500 PEPY.</p></div></details><details><summary><div class="acc-summary-inner"><h3 class="acc-summary-title">Is group health insurance cheaper than individual health insurance?</h3></div><span class="deep-acc-indicator" aria-hidden="true">+</span></summary><div class="deep-acc-body"><p>Yes. Group health insurance in India is typically 30 to 60 percent cheaper per rupee of cover than an equivalent individual retail health insurance policy, because insurers price group policies on pooled risk and because group policies have lower acquisition costs. Group policies also usually waive waiting periods and cover pre-existing conditions from day one, which retail policies do not.</p></div></details><details><summary><div class="acc-summary-inner"><h3 class="acc-summary-title">What is the minimum group size for group health insurance in India?</h3></div><span class="deep-acc-indicator" aria-hidden="true">+</span></summary><div class="deep-acc-body"><p>The IRDAI defines a group as any legally formed association of 7 or more members. Plum writes GHI policies starting at a minimum group size of 7 employees. Some insurers set higher minimums (10, 15, or 20 employees); others match the regulatory floor.</p></div></details><details><summary><div class="acc-summary-inner"><h3 class="acc-summary-title">Do group health insurance premiums increase every year?</h3></div><span class="deep-acc-indicator" aria-hidden="true">+</span></summary><div class="deep-acc-body"><p>Usually yes. Renewal pricing is driven by actual claims experience in the prior year, plus a market-wide medical inflation factor of 10 to 15 percent per year. A policy with a loss ratio below 60 percent might see a 5 to 15 percent renewal increase. A policy with a loss ratio above 85 percent can see renewal increases of 25 to 45 percent or more.</p></div></details><details><summary><div class="acc-summary-inner"><h3 class="acc-summary-title">Is GST applicable on group health insurance premiums?</h3></div><span class="deep-acc-indicator" aria-hidden="true">+</span></summary><div class="deep-acc-body"><p>Yes. GST on group health insurance premium is currently charged at 18 percent, and the GST portion is in addition to the premium amount. For a ₹10 lakh annual premium, the total invoice is ₹11.8 lakh including GST.</p></div></details><details><summary><div class="acc-summary-inner"><h3 class="acc-summary-title">Is group health insurance tax deductible for the employer?</h3></div><span class="deep-acc-indicator" aria-hidden="true">+</span></summary><div class="deep-acc-body"><p>Yes. Premium paid by the employer for employee group health insurance is deductible as a business expense under the Income Tax Act. GST paid on the premium is available as input tax credit for companies registered under GST, subject to the applicable rules.</p></div></details><details><summary><div class="acc-summary-inner"><h3 class="acc-summary-title">Can employees pay for group health insurance themselves?</h3></div><span class="deep-acc-indicator" aria-hidden="true">+</span></summary><div class="deep-acc-body"><p>Yes, through voluntary top-ups and dependent buy-ups structured as salary-deducted contributions. Employee-paid premium for self and family may qualify for Section 80D deduction under personal income tax, subject to the usual limits.</p></div></details><details><summary><div class="acc-summary-inner"><h3 class="acc-summary-title">What happens if an employee leaves the company mid-year?</h3></div><span class="deep-acc-indicator" aria-hidden="true">+</span></summary><div class="deep-acc-body"><p>The leaving employee's cover ends on the last working day (or on the policy exit date the employer defines). Some policies offer portability to an individual retail policy on exit, which the employee pays for. The employer typically does not receive a pro-rated refund.</p></div></details><details><summary><div class="acc-summary-inner"><h3 class="acc-summary-title">How is group health insurance different from ESIC?</h3></div><span class="deep-acc-indicator" aria-hidden="true">+</span></summary><div class="deep-acc-body"><p>ESIC is a statutory scheme for employees earning up to ₹21,000 per month, funded by employer and employee contributions, with access to ESIC-affiliated hospitals and dispensaries. Group health insurance is a private policy with broader hospital network choice and typically richer benefits. Many companies run both: ESIC for the covered segment and private GHI for the rest, or private GHI on top of ESIC for all employees.</p></div></details><details><summary><div class="acc-summary-inner"><h3 class="acc-summary-title">Can I include parents in group health insurance?</h3></div><span class="deep-acc-indicator" aria-hidden="true">+</span></summary><div class="deep-acc-body"><p>Yes. Parental cover is an add-on that most insurers support, structured as a floater or individual sum insured for one or both parents (or parents-in-law). Parental cover typically adds 40 to 120 percent to base premium depending on parent age bands and sum insured.</p></div></details></div></section>

  <section class="outside-lane cta" aria-labelledby="cta-h">
    <div class="section-label">Talk to our team</div>
    <h2 id="cta-h">Where to go from here</h2>
    <p>Every number on this page is a range because every GHI policy is a design decision. The right policy for a specific company is the one where premium, employee experience, and three-year cost trajectory match the company's budget, workforce, and values.</p>
<p>A specific quote for your company, built around your actual employee data and design preferences, is a conversation. Reach out to the Plum team at <a href="https://www.plumhq.com" rel="noopener">plumhq.com</a> to walk through the design choices that move the ranges on this page toward a number you can plan around.</p>
